## Cross-project vaults (2026-08-11) — usable, with scope rules

Beyond `fe-main` / `fe-ibero`, four MSCA/SVCM vaults are now in scope. **They are different evidence classes and must be labelled as such.**

| Vault | Class | Use for | Never use for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `MSCA/SVCM/reference/unesco` | **Normative instrument** (intergovernmental) | The **ethical obligation** to favour energy- and resource-efficient computing — see the energy note below | Empirical effect sizes; it measures nothing |
| `MSCA/SVCM/reference/horizon-europe` | **Funding/policy instrument** | Framing the *research proposal* (MSCA fellowship shape, evaluation criteria) | Curriculum evidence — it is not pedagogy research |
| `MSCA/SVCM/research/creative-practice` | Performance/archive theory + digital-heritage practice | The **vShowroom's conceptual layer**: what an archive of creative work is, and metadata for exhibiting it | FE pedagogy claims |
| `MSCA/SVCM/research/graphrag` | Technical KG/RAG literature | **The graph design itself** (TTOD visualisation, DevIAC architecture) | Any teaching or pedagogy claim |
| `MSCA/SVCM/research/computational-authorship` — Göbels et al. 2026, *Aligning AI with human values* (added 2026-08-13) | Peer-reviewed design-principles paper (Procedia CIRP, CIRP Design 2026) | **AI-assisted-coding pedagogy's design/ethics framing** (Unit 6) — human-centered AI design principles as the *why* behind ACCEPT/REJECT review discipline | A claim about front-end-specific outcomes — it's general HCI/AI-design, not web-specific |

### 🟢 The energy/climate gap is now grounded — normatively, not empirically

Declared open since 2026-08-10 and **closed at the right level** by the **UNESCO Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence** (`381137eng` coat, 307 nodes):

- node `630c7152` · p. 29 — Member States and enterprises *"should assess the direct and indirect environmental impact throughout the AI system life cycle, including, but not limited to, its **carbon footprint, energy consumption** and the environmental impact of raw material extraction"*
- node `17ccc7a8` · p. 30 — *"given the potential data-intensive or resource-intensive character of some of them… favour **data, energy and resource-efficient AI methods**"*
- node `7a02b8a9` · p. 18 — reduce environmental impact, precaution, ecosystem protection and restoration
- node `57481c7a` · p. 20 — AI *"can either benefit sustainability objectives or hinder their realization"*

**What changes:** the claim moves from *"reasoned commitment, no source"* to **"normatively grounded in an adopted international instrument."**

**What does NOT change:** UNESCO is a **Recommendation**, not a study. It supplies **obligation, not measurement**. There is still no vault source quantifying the carbon effect of front-end engineering choices, and its scope is **AI systems** — which covers this course's AI-assisted method and resource-intensive computing generally, **not** a direct front-end-to-carbon claim. **Invent no figures.**

> This is the O'Toole lesson applied in reverse. There, a title said *"Sustainable"* and the content was socio-technical — rejected. Here the content genuinely addresses energy and carbon, but the **document type** is normative — so it is accepted *as a norm*, not as evidence. Match the claim to the class of source.

